WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.175
What if it’s you in the water?

2
00:00:02.425 --> 00:00:04.500
Here’s what keeps you alive.

3
00:00:04.750 --> 00:00:06.900
First, a life jacket.

4
00:00:07.150 --> 00:00:12.725
It’s the difference between living and drowning, and you can’t put one on after you’ve gone in.

5
00:00:12.975 --> 00:00:16.050
Second, a rule called one, ten, one.

6
00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:20.900
One minute of cold shock, when you gasp and can’t control your breathing.

7
00:00:21.150 --> 00:00:24.900
Ten minutes of useful movement, before your muscles quit.

8
00:00:25.150 --> 00:00:29.150
And about one hour before hypothermia makes you unconscious.

9
00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:32.375
What does that tell you to do in the first minute?

10
00:00:33.375 --> 00:00:34.875
Nothing.

11
00:00:35.125 --> 00:00:37.650
Just float and get your breathing back.

12
00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:42.900
Florida’s water is warmer, which buys time, but not unlimited time.

13
00:00:43.150 --> 00:00:47.875
If you’re alone, take the HELP position: knees to your chest, arms crossed.

14
00:00:48.125 --> 00:00:50.325
In a group, huddle together.

15
00:00:50.575 --> 00:00:52.900
And stay with the boat if it floats.

16
00:00:53.150 --> 00:00:55.400
Don’t swim for a distant shore.

17
00:00:55.650 --> 00:00:57.925
It’s always farther than it looks.
